# /etc/crontab - system-wide scheduled jobs, read by /sbin/cron.
#
# This is the classic Linux system-crontab format. Unlike a per-user crontab,
# every job line carries the user name to run as. cron re-reads this file once
# a minute, so edits take effect without restarting the daemon.
#
# Environment for the jobs that follow (later assignments override earlier):
SHELL=/bin/shell
PATH=/sbin:/bin:/bin/x
HOME=/

# Job definition:
# .---------------- minute        (0 - 59)
# |  .------------- hour          (0 - 23)
# |  |  .---------- day of month  (1 - 31)
# |  |  |  .------- month         (1 - 12) or jan,feb,mar,apr ...
# |  |  |  |  .---- day of week   (0 - 6, Sunday=0 or 7) or sun,mon,tue ...
# |  |  |  |  |
# *  *  *  *  *  user  command to be executed
#
# A time field accepts '*', ranges 'a-b', steps '*/n' or 'a-b/n', and comma
# lists such as '0,30'. As in classic cron, when BOTH day-of-month and
# day-of-week are restricted the job runs when either one matches.
#
# The command runs through $SHELL, so pipes, redirection and '&&' work; its
# output is discarded (redirect it to a file to keep it). Examples:
#
# 0   *  *  *  *  root  /bin/df > /tmp/df.log           # top of every hour
# */5 *  *  *  *  misa  /bin/echo tick                  # every 5 minutes
# 30  6  *  *  sun  root /bin/date                       # 06:30 every Sunday
# 0   0  1  *  *  root /bin/echo "new month"             # midnight, 1st month
